      "text": "## Earth's Crust Composition"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#013",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:mineralogy:00-overview, git-history:mineralogy:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineralogy - Overview and Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nELEMENTAL ABUNDANCE IN EARTH'S CRUST (by mass)\n+------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  Oxygen (O)        46.6%   ← dominates all mineral anions  |\n|  Silicon (Si)      27.7%   ← forms silicate tetrahedra     |\n|  Aluminum (Al)      8.1%   ← feldspar, clays               |\n|  Iron (Fe)          5.0%   ← pyroxene, olivine, ore        |\n|  Calcium (Ca)       3.6%   ← plagioclase, calcite          |\n|  Sodium (Na)        2.8%   ← plagioclase, halite           |\n|  Potassium (K)      2.6%   ← K-feldspar, mica              |\n|  Magnesium (Mg)     2.1%   ← olivine, pyroxene, dolomite   |\n|  All others         1.5%   ← includes all ore metals       |\n+------------------------------------------------------------+\n   Si + O = 74.3% → silicates dominate by chemistry alone\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#014",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:mineralogy:00-overview, git-history:mineralogy:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineralogy - Overview and Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "This is why silicates are the dominant mineral class. The crustal abundance pattern also explains why Cu, Pb, Zn, Au are valuable — they're rare in the crust and concentrated only in specific ore deposit settings."
    },
