      "text": "## How Mineralogy Connects to Adjacent Fields"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#023",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:mineralogy:00-overview, git-history:mineralogy:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineralogy - Overview and Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nGEOLOGY / PETROLOGY\n(rock types, formation processes)\n         |          |\n         v\nMINERALOGY  ←————————→  MATERIALS SCIENCE\n(mineral identity,          (crystal structure = engineering\n structure, chemistry)       properties of metals, ceramics)\n         |          |\n    +---------------+\n    |               |\n    v             v\nCRYSTAL         GEOCHEMISTRY\nCHEMISTRY       (trace elements,\n(bonding,        isotopes, ore\n defects,        deposit models)\n symmetry)\n                  | |\n         v\n   ECONOMIC GEOLOGY\n   (ore deposit types,\n    critical mineral\n    supply chains)\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#024",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:mineralogy:00-overview, git-history:mineralogy:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineralogy - Overview and Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The 230 space groups are a direct application of finite group theory to 3D geometry: classifying all distinct ways a discrete symmetry group can act on Euclidean space subject to translational periodicity. The result (exactly 230, no more) was independently proven by Fedorov, Schoenflies, and Barlow in 1891–94 using pure group-theoretic enumeration — before X-rays were even discovered. Every mineral crystal is a physical instantiation of one of these 230 abstract symmetry classes."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#025",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:mineralogy:00-overview, git-history:mineralogy:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Mineralogy - Overview and Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**The mineralogy→materials bridge**: silicon carbide, alumina (Al₂O₃), spinel — all are minerals first, engineering ceramics second. The crystal structures studied in mineralogy are the same structures controlled in semiconductor manufacturing. Czochralski-grown silicon single crystals (the entire semiconductor industry substrate) are grown by the same principles as hydrothermal crystal growth; sapphire (corundum, Al₂O₃) wafers for LED substrates and phone covers are synthetic versions of a common mineral. The crystal orientation you cut matters for etch rates, defect propagation, and optical properties — all direct consequences of crystallographic symmetry."
    },
